Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_1.md. I judged from the diff and two short greps; I did not re-run the build or tests.

• Steps 1–2 are done as asked. The test is now post_hook_feedback_is_appended_after_the_tool_output_in_the_same_tool_result. Its doc comment and assert message no longer say "its own block", and the expected string is byte-identical. The failure output now prints signal= and yoyo's stdout, capped at 4096 bytes without splitting a character. A repo-wide grep finds the old name only in ARCHITECTURE.md, in the line that records it as the old name.
• Step 3: the cause was found, but not where the task guessed. The diff reports the flake reproduced in 48 of 60 runs under load, with yoyo killed by signal 13 (SIGPIPE). It was not the GroupKill hypothesis in src/tools.rs. yoyo uses the default SIGPIPE handling, and the test's post-hook never reads stdin. If the hook had already exited when yoyo wrote the hook's input, that write killed yoyo outright. The reported result after the fix is 0 of 60 under the same load. I did not re-run that loop myself.
• The fix is a real one, not an invented one. A new helper, write_all_no_sigpipe, is used at all three places that write to a child process's stdin: hooks, gh api in the PR review command, and the /copy clipboard. My grep found no other such writers in src/. Its tests re-run the test binary with yoyo's signal setting. One test shows that a plain write in the same setup dies by SIGPIPE, which proves the main test can tell the fix from its absence.
• Scope: the diff goes beyond "src/tools.rs only if the cause is there", into src/hooks.rs, two other writers and a new module. I accepted that because the measured cause lives in those files, and the change is recorded in ARCHITECTURE.md.
• Other checks: every new definition is used in the diff, and the module is declared in main.rs. libc was already a dependency. The size-gate entries for hooks.rs and commands_web.rs were updated. No config, CLI flag or startup behaviour changed.

One small risk I noted but did not fail it for: if a SIGPIPE was already pending before the write, it is left pending, so yoyo could still be killed when the helper unblocks it.
